  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\n- Unsettled With Several Chances of Showers and Thunderstorms \nThrough Thursday\n\nPersistent west-northwest flow with several embedded shortwaves will \nkeep a frontal boundary stalled south of the area over the next \nseveral days. Each shortwave will drive the potential for showers \nand thunderstorms until late Thursday into Friday a pattern shift \nforces the front south.\n\nFirst chance is this afternoon where the NAMNEST, which is best \nhandling ongoing convection, suggests an isolated pop-up \nthunderstorm or two. Coverage given little mesoscale forcing will be \nwidely isolated with many if not all staying dry.\n\nNext chance arrives tonight with the low-level jet just south of the \narea. This focuses the best convective potential south of the area, \nthough a few showers and storms are possible closer to the front \nalong and south of the I-94 corridor.\n\nAttention then turns to Tuesday afternoon where a complex of storms \nis forecast to either just miss us to the south, or impact our \nsouthwest forecast area. Best chance if it does reach us is south of \na Grand Haven to Lansing line. With ample deep layer shear (40+ \nkts) and MUCAPE values north of 2000 J/kg, this complex may have \nthe potential for damaging winds if it impacts our forecast area. \nThis matches the signal seen in several different machine learning\nguidance tools. However, storm track depends highly on how storms\nevolve tonight so confidence in any severe weather threat is low.\n\nWednesday and Thursday will feature additional afternoon/evening \nthunderstorm chances as weak shortwaves interact with the front \nsouth of the area. Best chance will be south of I-96, particularly \nnear I-94. The front then is driven south Friday ending the \nunsettled pattern briefly. There also may be locally heavy rain \nwith any of these rounds, with isolated maximum totals aoa 2 \ninches shown in HREF/REFS LPMM guidance.\n\n- More Widespread Rainfall Potential This Weekend\n\nFriday looks to be dry as surface and upper-level ridging\nsuppresses convective potential. However, that pattern changes by\nSaturday. This weekend, potentially as early as Friday Night, a \nmid-level wave ejects into the Central Great Lakes driving a \nsurface low across the area. This will bring more widespread \nprecipitation until the low exits late weekend with cooler \ntemperatures with it. &&",
